  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 247 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: An upper-level low builds into the region tomorrow and \nremains over the area into Thursday. An associated surface low will \nform over the Great Lakes and occlude as it moves into New York and \nNew England, bringing consistent showers. The showers enter northern \nNew York tomorrow morning and enter Vermont tomorrow afternoon as a \nsouth to north oriented line. There will be some convective \nenhancement on the frontside, due to surface CAPE reaching up to \naround 1,000 J. A lack of deep-layer is expected to prevent any \nsevere threat. These storms will likely have the highest rainfall \nrates of the entire event, but dry antecedent conditions and some \nsouth to north storm motion should keep the flood threat relatively \nlow for the day on Tuesday. The axis of rain looks to become more \nsse-nnw oriented heading into Tuesday evening and night, and east to \nsoutheast flow will develop. While the precipitation should trend \ntoward more stratiform as the night goes on, some convective \nenhancement will still be likely, especially in southern Vermont \nwhere the highest totals are expected. This Tuesday night into \nWednesday morning would likely have the highest concern for any \nflash flooding as the axis of rain looks to stay relatively \nstationary. Heading through Wednesday, the dynamics weaken and the \naxis of rain looks to become more broken and lighter. Periods of \nshowers will continue Wednesday night through Thursday, but they \nwill become much lighter and gradually less widespread as the \nsurface low weakens and pushes east. The area of highest concern is \nthe eastern slopes and spine of the southern Green Mountains where \nthe best dynamics will combine with upsloping. The east slopes of \nthe Adirondacks will likely be the second area of focus due to the \nupsloping. Still, in the more favored areas, only isolated flash \nflooding looks to be possible. The Champlain Valley and Northeast \nKingdom will likely receive some locally lower totals due to \ndownsloping.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: An unsettled weather pattern will continue into the \nweekend as our approaching system slowly departs Friday with an \nadditional system possibly arriving sometime late into the weekend. \nThere is still a large spread in timing of the departure of our \napproaching system and the weekend system, but trends have moved \ntowards lingering showers and embedded thunderstorms Friday, with a \nmore delayed trend in the weekend activity, perhaps closer towards \nMonday next week. Models denote cyclonic circulation lingering to \nour south and east Friday with continued wrap around moisture in \nportions of eastern Vermont. With easterly flow blocked against the \nGreens, a differential heating boundary between the cooler eastern \nVermont and warmer Champlain Valley and northern New York may set \nup. This will lead to areas of shower development along the \nconvergence boundary Friday afternoon potentially.\n\nWeak ridging tries to build into our cwa for Saturday, before \nanother shortwave, associated with a 700-500mb trof approaches from \nthe Great Lakes. Model ensembles in the GEFS and GEPS denote a more \ndelayed system evolution closer to Sunday or Monday, with more \nisolated to scattered terrain showers Saturday afternoon. This is \nline with abundance of subsidence that may be across the area as our \ncurrent system occludes and subsides over southern New England late \nthis week. Latest guidances shows Saturday highest potential over \nnorthern NY, while Sunday activity wl be more widespread impacting \nboth VT and NY. Limited run to run continuity and large spread in \npotential solutions results in low forecaster confidence and \nassociated predictability on probability and timing of precip, \nespecially Sunday into early next week. Temps will be driven by \nclouds/precip, but mostly above normal values anticipated, \nespecially at night with lows in the upper 50s to upper 60s, while \nhighs range from the mid 70s to mid 80s. Some levels of humidity are \nexpected, but the probability of much above normal temps or \nsignificant heat is low given the anticipated pattern.",
